mod std.jvm

;; ── JVM / Java interop capability ──────────────────────
;;
;; The `JVM` effect is a *capability marker* — it has no operations.
;; Any Java interop site (`Class/method`, `Class/staticField`,
;; constructor `Class/new`, dot-access on a Java reference) requires
;; "JVM" to be in the surrounding fn's effect row.
;;
;; Top-level scripts inherit ambient permissions, so they're exempt.
;; Annotated `fn`s that touch Java must declare `::: JVM`.
;;
;; Why this exists: Irij's effect rows would otherwise be a lie —
;; arbitrary `java.io.*` / `java.util.Random` / `System/exit` calls
;; could happen inside a fn declared "pure". Tracking every Java
;; method's purity is intractable across JDK versions, so the design
;; takes a hard line: *all* Java interop is impure unless explicitly
;; declared. Stdlib effects (Random, FileIO, Time, Db, …) wrap common
;; Java surface so most code never needs `JVM` directly.
;;
;; Usage:
;;
;;   fn parse-int :: Str Int ::: JVM
;;     (s -> java.lang.Long/parseLong s)
;;
;;   parse-int "42"          ;; top level has every effect
;;
;; A library keeps Java behind a cap instead: a `pub effect` for the
;; resource, `cap instant :: Clock = "java.time.Instant"`, and a real
;; handler `system-clock :: Clock ::: Clock` whose clauses call the cap.
;; A cap call performs the cap's effect, so whoever installs the handler
;; declares `::: Clock`, never JVM. JVM needs no import to be named in a
;; row.

pub effect JVM

;; DEPRECATED — will be removed. It used to grant JVM to a block inside
;; a fn that didn't declare it, which let a fn declared pure call Java.
;; A `with` now needs its handler's row, and this one's is JVM, so
;; installing it grants nothing; using it prints a warning. Declare
;; `::: JVM` on the fn that calls Java instead.
pub handler unsafe-jvm :: JVM ::: JVM
